Code and data in Rceattle that come from other projects
=======================================================

Rceattle began as a TMB implementation of CEATTLE, the ADMB multispecies model
written by Kirstin Holsman, James Ianelli and Kerim Aydin (Holsman et al. 2016).
It has since grown into a general age-structured assessment package (one or
many species, one or two sexes, age- and length-based data, estimated growth,
environmental linkages, diagnostics, simulation and management strategy
evaluation). The pieces below come from other projects and keep their original
licenses. Each entry names who holds copyright in what was taken; the main
authors are also listed as contributors in DESCRIPTION.


Code adapted from other projects
--------------------------------

src/TMB/comp_osa.hpp
    One-step-ahead forms of the multinomial and Dirichlet-multinomial, from two
    sources:
    - distr.hpp in OSA_multivariate_dists
      (https://github.com/vtrijoulet/OSA_multivariate_dists), the reference code
      for Trijoulet et al. (2023): dmultinom_osa() and ddirmultinom_osa() keep
      its names and its CDF terms. "Copyright (c) 2022, Vanessa Trijoulet,
      Christoffer Moesgaard Albertsen, Kasper Kristensen, Chris M. Legault,
      Timothy J. Miller, and Anders Nielsen. All rights reserved." That
      repository states no license.
    - WHAM, src/age_comp_osa.hpp (src/age_comp_osa.h in current WHAM; Tim
      Miller; https://github.com/timjmiller/wham), GPL-3, which adapts the same
      code.
    osa_order() is Anders Nielsen's order(), in both.

src/TMB/helper_functions.hpp: ddirmultinom()
    From WHAM, src/age_comp_osa.hpp (GPL-3); identical in distr.hpp above.

src/TMB/helper_functions.hpp: construct_Q()
    From Matthew Cheng's GMRF_WAA (https://github.com/chengmatt/GMRF_WAA), the code
    for Cheng et al. (2023). That repository states no license.

src/TMB/growth.hpp (the growth functions and the length_sd_at_age() and
fill_age_length_key() helpers split from them) and R/8-sim_mod.R: get_growth_matrix_r()
    Adapted from Giancarlo Correa's growth code in WHAM's growth branch
    (https://github.com/timjmiller/wham/tree/growth, at commit 16770d4): the
    mean length-at-age block of src/wham_v0.cpp and pred_LAA() in
    src/helper_functions.hpp. GPL-3.

src/TMB/predation.hpp: calculate_vulnerability(), calculate_parametric_suitability()
    The multinomial-logistic vulnerability transform and the size-ratio
    suitability scaled to a peak of 1, of Trijoulet et al. (2020), following
    MS_SSM.cpp in https://github.com/vtrijoulet/Multisp_model_JAE (Vanessa
    Trijoulet). That repository states no license.

R/6-phaser.R: TMBphase()
    Adapted from TMBphase by Gavin Fay
    (https://github.com/kaskr/TMB_contrib_R/tree/master/TMBphase). GPL-2.

R/6-tmb_aic.R: TMBAIC(); R/0-tmb_helpers.R: .fit_tmb()
    TMBAIC() is copied from TMBhelper::TMBAIC() by James Thorson
    (https://github.com/kaskr/TMB_contrib_R/tree/master/TMBhelper), GPL-3. The
    fallback in .fit_tmb() mirrors TMBhelper::fit_tmb()'s Newton steps,
    diagnostics table and convergence messages.

R/7-plot_ceattle.R: rich.colors.short()
    rich.colors() from gplots, by Arni Magnusson (GPL-2), as shortened in r4ss
    by Ian G. Taylor (GPL-3).

tests/comparison/WHAM-growth-comparison.R, tests/comparison/WHAM-OSA-comparison.R,
tests/comparison/WHAM_growth_comparison/helper.R and case3.xlsx, data/whamGrowthData.rda
    Modified from case 3 of the WHAM growth examples by Giancarlo Correa and Jane
    Sullivan for the Ocean Frontier Institute's 2023 workshop
    (https://giancarlomcorrea.netlify.app/post/ofi-workshop/). No license stated.

tests/comparison/WHAM_growth_comparison/res_tables/par_tables.Rmd
    WHAM's inst/contribute/par_tables.Rmd template, written out by WHAM's
    plot_wham_output(). GPL-3.

Standard idioms and build templates
    - src/TMB/helper_functions.hpp: rho_trans(), from the TMB examples
      (https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp, GPL-2); max2(), from the tmb-users list
      (https://groups.google.com/g/tmb-users/c/QAVmgj66OC0); posfun() and
      first_difference(), ADMB's functions in their usual TMB form (ADMB,
      https://github.com/admb-project/admb, BSD license).
    - src/TMB/ceattle.cpp: LOM_t, TMB's list-of-matrices idiom.
    - src/Makevars, src/Makevars.win, src/init_dummy_file.cpp and the start of
      src/TMB/compile.R: the TMBtools templates (Martin Lysy;
      https://github.com/mlysy/TMBtools, GPL (>= 2)).


Methods taken from other assessment models and packages
-------------------------------------------------------

These were written for Rceattle from each project's equations, documentation
and code. The source is cited in the code where each is used.

Stock Synthesis (Methot and Wetzel 2013; https://github.com/nmfs-ost/ss3-source-code,
CC0-1.0): the DoubleNormalSS3 selectivity, growth CV patterns, plus-group mean
length, the exponential environmental link on catchability, initial equilibrium
catch and the InitF convention.

AMAK (https://github.com/afsc-assessments/amak; Copyright (c) 2014 NMFS-toolbox),
ebswp (https://github.com/afsc-assessments/ebswp; Copyright (c) 2026 Jim Ianelli)
and rtmb_ebswp (https://github.com/jimianelli/rtmb_ebswp; Copyright (c) 2026 Jim
Ianelli), all MIT-licensed: the AFSC multinomial composition likelihood, the
non-parametric selectivity penalties (the smooth shape penalty follows
